Get assistance on data on Singapore's economy and socio-demography using semantic search and Large Language Models. \",\"logo\":\"494a60dc-f416-4a00-a859-e48a2c3e7caa\",\"link\":{\"type\":\"external\",\"url\":\"https://www.singstat.gov.sg/chatwithsandra-beta\",\"internal_id\":\"\",\"internal_label\":\"\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"\"},\"label_link\":\"Find out more\",\"sort\":1,\"is_opened\":false,\"url_type\":\"url\"},{\"title\":\"SingStat Model Context Protocol (MCP)\",\"description\":\"Gain AI-ready access to Singapore’s official data through the SingStat MCP. It enables query and retrieval of Singapore’s official data while using other AI chatbots and AI-powered applications.\",\"logo\":\"9cc73add-bc81-4cba-a62d-c9e26ba754bf\",\"link\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"da1f22b8-3ecc-402f-a509-691354bb42ce\",\"internal_label\":\"SingStat Model Context Protocol (MCP)\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"url_type\":\"url\",\"file\":null,\"label_link\":\"Find out more\",\"sort\":1,\"is_opened\":true},{\"title\":\"Business Insights Tool for Enterprises (BITE)\",\"description\":\"Gain insights on customers, industry and business performance through interactive dashboards and benchmark my performance tool. \",\"logo\":\"88fce5ff-9ad0-47f2-81c0-2e4d08cc5ba2\",\"link\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"47cc07f1-911c-44c7-bcdc-5451cc0b921d\",\"internal_label\":\"Business Insights Tool for Enterprises (BITE)\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"label_link\":\"Find out more\",\"sort\":1,\"is_opened\":false,\"url_type\":\"url\"},{\"title\":\"Singapore Standard Occupational Classification (SSOC) Search\",\"description\":\"Search occupational codes based on Singapore Standard Occupational Classification (SSOC 2024) edition. \",\"logo\":\"03d96d4a-3071-4772-8431-cc6a980680e2\",\"link\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"ce3e3578-780b-427e-a023-2a0c6c712ea4\",\"internal_label\":\"SSOC Search\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"label_link\":\"Find out more\",\"sort\":1,\"is_opened\":false,\"url_type\":\"url\"},{\"title\":\"Anonymised Microdata Access Programme (AMAP) \",\"description\":\"Access anonymised microdata in secure settings. Researchers holding full-time appointments at Singapore's Autonomous Universities can apply. \",\"logo\":\"8eff7117-ab51-4749-a2bb-c0d8179507ac\",\"link\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"bd1e1846-d869-4070-8963-4cfe3ff28479\",\"internal_label\":\"Anonymised Microdata Access Programme (AMAP)\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"label_link\":\"Find out more\",\"sort\":1,\"is_opened\":false,\"url_type\":\"url\"},{\"title\":\"Participating in Our Surveys \",\"description\":\"Browse the list of major surveys of businesses and households conducted by DOS and other public sector agencies. \",\"logo\":\"\",\"link\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"40f7389b-1c11-45e7-9665-0d7990c68003\",\"internal_label\":\"Participate in our surveys\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"label_link\":\"Find out more\",\"sort\":1,\"is_opened\":false,\"url_type\":\"url\"},{\"title\":\"Sampling Services for Household Survey\",\"description\":\"Get assistance on sample design and selection at a charge.\",\"logo\":\"\",\"link\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"da5d24b3-0db7-4bf7-93f9-3f294cea5da7\",\"internal_label\":\"Sampling Service for Household Surveys\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"label_link\":\"Find out more\",\"sort\":1,\"is_opened\":false,\"url_type\":\"url\"}]}},\"sort\":4},{\"id\":\"mfhjfmk3\",\"collection\":\"latest_news_publications\",\"name\":\"Latest News Publication\",\"category\":\"Home Component\",\"sort\":5,\"item\":{\"id\":\"mfhjfmk3\",\"name\":\"Latest News Publications\",\"placeholder_definition\":{\"id\":\"Main\",\"name\":\"Main\"},\"widget_definition\":{\"name\":\"latest_news_publications\"},\"parameters\":{\"calendar_link\":1,\"default_open_tab\":\"news\",\"news_link\":3,\"publications_link\":7},\"collection\":\"latest_news_publications\",\"sort\":5,\"category\":\"Home Component\"}},{\"id\":\"mek4c4ue\",\"collection\":\"let_us_explain\",\"category\":\"Home Component\",\"sort\":6,\"item\":{\"id\":\"mek4c4ue\",\"name\":\"Let us Explain\",\"placeholder_definition\":{\"id\":\"Main\",\"name\":\"Main\"},\"widget_definition\":{\"name\":\"let_us_explain\"},\"parameters\":{\"section_title\":\"Let Us Explain\",\"insights\":[{\"title\":\"How Are Population Estimates Compiled?\",\"url_type\":\"file\",\"url\":{\"type\":\"\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"\",\"internal_label\":\"\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"file\":\"8268fb2f-6c43-493a-84dc-580d9f80ff76\",\"description\":\"Learn about the importance of population estimates and how it is compiled in Singapore.\",\"image\":\"3bb883cc-107c-472f-9412-5a6441172862\"},{\"title\":\"Personal Disposable Income vs Household Market Income\",\"url_type\":\"file\",\"url\":{\"type\":\"\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"\",\"internal_label\":\"\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"file\":\"3018444e-ec2c-4b3f-836a-5126ad1bb9c1\",\"description\":\"What are the key differences between Personal Disposable Income and Household Market Income?\",\"image\":\"51f67dcb-b10f-4fbe-80f5-3d28bdc4e4f2\"},{\"title\":\"CPI Explained\",\"url\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_label\":\"How Does Hosting Formula (F1) Benefit Singapore's Economy\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"description\":\"Why does the reported inflation differ from my personal experience?\",\"image\":\"1bc0b5f1-7670-4fe3-926d-1f687f9b1a1e\",\"url_type\":\"file\",\"file\":\"ec333809-011c-48f1-8807-7ef816c9bae4\"},{\"title\":\"What is Shrinkflation?\",\"url_type\":\"file\",\"url\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"eb93972c-f560-4c57-a128-51ccf97b84ea\",\"internal_label\":\"What is Shrinkflation?\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"file\":\"61144ec7-2081-4da1-91ae-56a5ccb36ea5\",\"description\":\"An explanation of hidden price increases with examples of products most affected by shrinkflation.\",\"image\":\"0c3e8430-87c7-4d01-a3d1-eeb068a5892f\"},{\"title\":\"Growing Beyond Borders\",\"url\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_label\":\"How does internationalisation contribution to Singapore Infographic\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"description\":\"How does internationalisation contribute to Singapore?\",\"image\":\"391a460d-d409-4d61-b2a0-aee0cc731d2e\",\"url_type\":\"file\",\"file\":\"f6616ac6-ce6b-468b-ac93-3c026e5c5dea\"},{\"title\":\"The Role of Trade in Economic Growth\",\"url_type\":\"file\",\"url\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"ddeea678-ac07-402f-8d16-a4472854352e\",\"internal_label\":\"How does Trade Contribute to Output-based GDP?\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"file\":\"23278488-7b33-4c23-a4fe-a9f78f00d413\",\"description\":\"Find out how trade plays a vital role in shaping Singapore’s economic landscape.\",\"image\":\"82efd65d-81a6-40c6-8479-66955b3f88cc\"},{\"title\":\"Changes to the Key Household Income Trends 2025\",\"url\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_label\":\"How Does Hosting Formula (F1) Benefit Singapore's Economy\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"description\":\"Find out more about the changes to the income and household coverage in the Key Household Income Trends report.\",\"image\":\"aa33730e-d2e5-4659-9b5c-b69fc704d3ba\",\"url_type\":\"file\",\"file\":\"c2cc2968-0601-4fb1-be16-735e710e6666\"},{\"title\":\"Household Sector Balance Sheet vs Household Wealth Estimates\",\"url_type\":\"file\",\"url\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"\",\"internal_label\":\"\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"file\":\"f4408efa-2d36-4d76-b9b1-67480eeecc2c\",\"description\":\"What are the key differences between the Household Sector Balance Sheet and Household Wealth Estimates?\",\"image\":\"28534422-737a-4dce-a1c8-ba5b340ecb4d\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding Wholesale Trade\",\"url\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_label\":\"Wholesale Trade: A Sectoral Analysis\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"description\":\"A sectoral analysis of the Wholesale Trade industry.\",\"image\":\"4fc1d98c-bb3f-416d-a7b5-e7bac536ab50\",\"url_type\":\"file\",\"file\":\"8a5672f5-837f-4378-97e3-398209e3897d\"},{\"title\":\"GDP Explained\",\"url\":{\"type\":\"internal\",\"url\":\"\",\"internal_id\":\"fedae35c-1105-4cd8-bcd0-5e9d68182451\",\"internal_label\":\"Old Age Support Ratio\",\"fieldType\":\"InputLink\",\"collection_source\":\"xtr_cms_pages\"},\"description\":\"What activities contribute to Singapore's GDP?\",\"image\":\"879b4af7-69b9-4199-bc99-ea618a830b24\",\"url_type\":\"file\",\"file\":\"9408491a-8b57-4276-970c-5e4fa5032538\"}],\"limit_entries\":true,\"max_entries\":6,\"let_us_explain_link\":42},\"collection\":\"let_us_explain\",\"sort\":3,\"category\":\"Home Component\"}}]},\"content\":null,\"summary\":\"\u003cdiv\u003e\\n\u003cdiv\u003eOfficial statistics and data from Singapore Department of Statistics. Find the latest economic indicators, population data, and statistical reports.\u003c/div\u003e\\n\u003c/div\u003e\",\"navigation_title\":null,\"show_in_navigation\":false,\"full_path\":\"/home\",\"metadata\":\"{\\n\\t\\\"title\\\":\\\"Singapore Department of Statistics\\\",\\n\\t\\\"description\\\":\\\"Singapore's National Statistical Office that collects, compiles and disseminates economic and socio-demographic statistics.\\\",\\n\\t\\\"keywords\\\":[],\\n\\t\\\"searchSG\\\":{\\n\\t\\t\\\"contentType\\\":\\\"Homepage\\\",\\n\\t\\t\\\"topic\\\":[],\\n\\t\\t\\\"section\\\":[]\\n\\t}\\n}\",\"page_image\":null,\"seo_description\":null,\"page_tags\":null,\"redirect\":false,\"url\":\"/\",\"layout\":\"5d0acb08-6f1c-48f5-bd8d-31adb934b5bb\",\"wysiwyg_configuration\":null,\"breadcrumb_title\":null,\"published_at\":\"2026-09-30T03:00:00.000Z\",\"enable_searchsg_description\":true,\"unpublished_at\":null,\"parent\":null},\"draftMode\":false,\"searchParams\":\"$24:props:searchParams\",\"whitelistedDomains\":\"$undefined\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"49:I[81665,[\"9690\",\"static/chunks/8cc6faea-06177138421f2356.js\",\"4390\",\"static/chunks/d648eb28-05c9445a2e0b2734.js\",\"4935\",\"static/chunks/e37a0b60-b9701474b4598bbe.js\",\"2953\",\"static/chunks/2953-cfcdd0e78cbb430a.js\",\"4621\",\"static/chunks/4621-3806689a9f4b4c12.js\",\"4349\",\"static/chunks/4349-e68eac827aefc7ad.js\",\"558\",\"static/chunks/558-85f09fc80154cc6c.js\",\"8892\",\"static/chunks/8892-6f010de7520a6730.js\",\"8974\",\"static/chunks/app/page-603c0f7eb294d92b.js\"],\"default\"]\n4a:T652,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eKey Highlights\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSingapore’s net asset position in the IIP rose 5.2% quarter-on-quarter from $1,499 billion to $1,577 billion as at end 2Q 2026. This was due to a larger increase in external assets compared to that of external liabilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAs at end 2Q 2026, portfolio investment and other investment registered net asset positions of $2,376 billion and $148 billion respectively. Reserve assets stood at $551 billion. In contrast, direct investment and financial derivatives registered net liability positions of $1,476 billion and $23 billion respectively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eAbout the International Investment Position\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe International Investment Position (IIP) provides a snapshot of the financial assets and liabilities that residents of an economy have with the rest of the world. The net IIP, which is the difference between an economy’s external financial assets and liabilities, reflects an economy’s state of financial stability and net external wealth.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe analysis of the IIP is important to Singapore given its status as an international financial centre and trading hub. Singapore has been registering net asset positions in the IIP (i.e., net positive IIP), with consistently higher external assets over external liabilities, making it a net creditor country to the rest of the world.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eExternal assets and liabilities comprise various functional categories, namely: direct investment, portfolio investment, financial derivatives, other investment and reserve assets (external assets only).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eLinks to Resources\u003c/h2\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"4b:T6c2,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eKey Highlights\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCPI in Aug 2026 rose 0.6% from the previous month and 2.3% from a year earlier.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCPI-All Items inflation edged up to 2.3% y-o-y in Aug 2026, from 2.2% in Jul 2026 due to higher inflation for airfares (12.9%), point-to-point transport services (12.3%), clothing \u0026amp; footwear (3.1%), personal care products (2.7%), as well as food (2.3%).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eAbout the Consumer Price Index (CPI)\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe CPI is designed to measure the average price changes of a fixed basket of consumption goods and services commonly purchased by resident households over time. The CPI is widely used and analysed as a measure of consumer price inflation. It measures price movements (i.e., change in prices) but not absolute price levels at a point in time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe weighting pattern for the 2024-based CPI was derived from the expenditure values obtained from the Household Expenditure Survey (HES) 2023, and updated to 2024 values by taking into account price changes between 2023 and 2024.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe CPI covers only household consumption expenditure incurred by resident households, which refer to households where the household reference person is a Singapore Citizen or Permanent Resident. It excludes non-consumption expenditure such as loan repayments, income taxes, purchases of houses, shares, and other financial assets etc.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA total of 6,800 brands/ varieties are included in the 2024-based CPI basket and they are classified into ten main expenditure divisions based largely on the Classification of Individual Consumption According to Purpose (COICOP). The total number of outlets selected for pricing is about 4,500.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eLinks to Resources\u003c/h2\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"4c:Ta28,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eKey Highlights\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003ch3 class=\"text-h4\"\u003eRetail Trade\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRetail sales rose 1.5% in July 2026 on a year-on-year basis, moderating from the 4.0% growth in June 2026. Excluding Motor Vehicles, Parts \u0026amp; Accessories, retail sales increased 1.5%, compared to the 4.1% growth in June 2026. On a seasonally adjusted basis, retail sales rose 0.9% in July 2026 over the previous month. Excluding Motor Vehicles, Parts \u0026amp; Accessories, seasonally adjusted retail sales increased 0.7% compared to June 2026.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWithin the Retail Trade sector, retailers of Recreational Goods and Watches \u0026amp; Jewellery recorded year-on-year sales growth of 13.9% and 11.1% respectively in July 2026, mainly driven by higher sales of sporting goods and jewellery. Similarly, sales of Computer and Telecommunications Equipment rose 5.1%. In contrast, the Food \u0026amp; Alcohol and Department Stores industries recorded year-on-year declines in sales of 5.1% and 3.5% respectively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3 class=\"text-h4\"\u003eFood \u0026amp; Beverage Services\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSales of Food \u0026amp; Beverage (F\u0026amp;B) services declined 1.9% in July 2026 on a year-on-year basis, following the 2.3% decrease in June 2026. On a seasonally adjusted basis, sales of F\u0026amp;B services rose 0.6% in July 2026 compared to the previous month.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWithin the F\u0026amp;B services sector, Food Courts \u0026amp; Other Eating Places as well as Cafes registered decreases in sales of 6.6% and 6.4% respectively. Similarly, Restaurants declined 0.3% during this period. In contrast, turnover of Fast Food Outlets and Food Caterers rose 4.6% and 0.3% in July 2026.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eAbout the Retail Sales Index and Food \u0026amp; Beverage Services Index\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Retail Sales Index (RSI) and Food \u0026amp; Beverage Services Index (FSI) measure the monthly sales performance of the Retail Trade and Food \u0026amp; Beverage (F\u0026amp;B) services industries based on the sales records of Retail Trade and F\u0026amp;B services establishments respectively. Sales figure refers to the value of retail goods or food \u0026amp; beverages sold to consumers during the month, excluding taxes on products such as Goods and Services Tax (GST), Additional Registration Fee (ARF) and Certificates of Entitlement (COE).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe RSI and FSI are available at current prices and in volume terms. The indices at current prices measure the changes of sales values which can result from changes in both price and quantity, while the indices in volume terms measure the changes in the volume of economic activity by removing the price effect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eLinks to Resources\u003c/h2\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"4d:Tbcd,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eKey Highlights\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003ch3 class=\"text-h4\"\u003eSingapore Manufactured Products Price Index\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Singapore Manufactured Products Price Index (SMPPI) rose 4.4% month-on-month in Aug 2026, following the 2.4% increase in Jul 2026. The Oil index rose 3.8%, extending the 4.4% increase in the previous month. The Non-oil index increased 4.5% in Aug 2026, up from the 2.1% increase in Jul 2026, continuing the increases seen in recent months.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn a year-on-year basis, the SMPPI rose 40.1% in Aug 2026, up from the 35.4% increase in Jul 2026. The Oil index continued to rise, increasing 48.2%. The Non-oil index rose 38.9%, marking a further increase from preceding months.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3 class=\"text-h4\"\u003eDomestic Supply Price Index\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Domestic Supply Price Index (DSPI) rose 4.1% month-on-month in Aug 2026, extending the 2.4% increase in Jul 2026. The Oil index increased 4.7%, up from the 3.2% increase in Jul 2026, following three consecutive months of decline from Apr to Jun 2026. The Non-oil index rose 3.9%, up from the 2.1% increase in Jul 2026.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn a year-on-year basis, the DSPI rose 38.8% in Aug 2026, extending the increases seen in recent months. The Oil index continued to rise, increasing 57.4%, up from the 56.8% gain in Jul 2026. The Non-oil index rose 33.0%, following the 29.3% increase in Jul 2026, with progressively stronger growth recorded in recent months.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe month-on-month and year-on-year increases in the SMPPI and DSPI Non-oil indices were mainly driven by higher prices of electrical machinery apparatus, such as electronic integrated circuits.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eAbout this Publication\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Singapore Manufactured Products Price Index (SMPPI) monitors price changes of locally manufactured commodities. The weights of the index are derived from the distribution of local production by commodity in 2022. Ex-factory prices are obtained from principal manufacturers of the items concerned.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Domestic Supply Price Index (DSPI) is designed to measure the changes in the price level of either goods manufactured locally or imported, which are retained for use in the domestic economy by the government, business or household sectors. For imported products, the prices refer to those paid by importers in bringing the goods into Singapore, commonly referred to as CIF prices. In the case of Singapore manufactured products, the quotations used are ex-factory prices. The weight for each commodity item is proportional to the value of its total supply or availability in the domestic market in 2022.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe 2023-based series is compiled using the 'modified Laspeyres' formula where the weight reference period of 2022 preceded the price reference period of 2023.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eLinks to Resources\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"/files/ef9d1136-41ed-4350-b7c9-60075fe674e2.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eFull Report\u003c/a\u003e \u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003ca href=\"/files/541212c5-dc2e-45da-8118-805b82954272.xls\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eExcel File\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"4e:Tb9e,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eKey Highlights\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003ch3 class=\"text-h4\"\u003eImport Price Index\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Import Price Index (IPI) rose 1.6% month-on-month in Aug 2026, extending the 0.9% increase in Jul 2026 and marking a second consecutive month of gains. The Oil index increased 4.8%, accelerating from the 1.9% rise in Jul 2026. The Non-oil index rose 0.4%, continuing its recent run of monthly increases of less than 1%.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn a year-on-year basis, the IPI rose 15.6% in Aug 2026, up from the 13.5% increase in Jul 2026. The Oil index rose 56.6%, up from the 45.8% increase in Jul 2026. The Non-oil index rose 4.8%, unchanged from the increase in July 2026, following a gradual pick-up in the preceding months.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3 class=\"text-h4\"\u003eExport Price Index\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Export Price Index (EPI) rose 1.9% month-on-month in Aug 2026, its first increase in four months. The Oil index increased 7.1%, accelerating from the 0.7% rise in Jul 2026. The Non-oil index rose 0.3%, reversing the 0.2% dip in the previous month.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn a year-on-year basis, the EPI rose 15.2% in Aug 2026, up from the 13.1% increase in Jul 2026. The Oil index continued to rise, increasing 51.6%. The Non-oil index rose 7.2%, similar to the 7.3% increase in Jul 2026.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe month-on-month and year-on-year increases in the IPI and EPI Oil indices were mainly due to higher prices of petroleum and petroleum products. The increases in the IPI and EPI Non-oil indices were mainly due to higher prices of electrical machinery apparatus such as electronic integrated circuits. Higher prices of Miscellaneous Manufactured Articles, particularly jewellery, also contributed to both the month-on-month and year-on-year increases in the IPI Non-oil index.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eAbout this Publication\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Import Price Index (IPI) is used primarily as an indicator to track the price trends of imported goods into Singapore. Prices of imports, valued at CIF terms (cost, insurance and freight) are obtained monthly from major importers. The weighting pattern of the index is derived from import statistics for 2023.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Export Price Index (EPI) measures changes in the price of all exports of merchandise from Singapore, including re-exports. In general, prices are obtained from major exporters of the selected commodities. Prices used in the index computation, are the prices of goods that physically leave Singapore’s main ports of export; also termed as Free on Board (FOB) prices. The weights of the index are derived from the distribution of export by commodity in 2023.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe 2023-based series is compiled using the Laspeyres formula where the price and weight reference periods are aligned to the year 2023.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eLinks to Resources\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"/files/c9f5fdb5-b4fc-4fd5-9321-278c3672e4d1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eFull Report\u003c/a\u003e \u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003ca href=\"/files/8ef03dd3-134f-4b4f-9b80-9c97e689c96d.xls\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eExcel File\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"4f:Tf8c,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eKey Highlights\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003ch4 class=\"text-h5\"\u003ePopulation\u003c/h4\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSingapore's total population stood at 6.21 million as at end-June 2026, a 1.6% increase from end-June 2025, while the resident population grew by 0.6% to 4.23 million over the same period. Singapore's resident population continued to age. The median age of the resident population rose to 43.6 years, and the proportion of residents aged 65 years and over rose to 19.5% in 2026 from 12.4% in 2016. The ethnic composition of the resident population remained broadly stable over the decade, comprising 73.9% Chinese, 13.5% Malays, 9.0% Indians, and 3.6% Others as at end-June 2026.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch4 class=\"text-h5\"\u003eGeographical Distribution\u003c/h4\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMore than half (51.7%) of the 4.23 million residents resided in nine out of the fifty-five planning areas. Tampines was the most populous, with 296,060 residents. Outram, Ang Mo Kio, Jurong East, Bukit Merah and Rochor were among the top few planning areas with the highest proportions of residents aged 65 years and over, while Tengah, Punggol and Tampines had the highest proportion of residents aged below 5 years old. An estimated 3.20 million residents, or approximately three in four, resided in HDB flats.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch4 class=\"text-h5\"\u003eHouseholds and Housing\u003c/h4\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe number of resident households increased 1.6% from 1.46 million in 2024 to 1.49 million in 2025. The average household size decreased from 3.39 persons in 2015 to 3.06 persons in 2025. While married couple-based households with children remained the most prevalent living arrangement, their share declined from 56.5% in 2015 to 47.6% in 2025. One-person households rose from 11.9% to 16.7% over the same period.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch4 class=\"text-h5\"\u003eFamily Formation and Dissolution\u003c/h4\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA total of 24,688 civil and Muslim marriages were registered in 2025, 6.2% lower than the year before. The median age at first marriage was 31.1 years for grooms and 29.6 years for brides in 2025, up from 30.3 and 28.2 years a decade ago. A total of 7,242 marriages ended in a divorce or an annulment in 2025, a decrease of 1.9% from the previous year.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch4 class=\"text-h5\"\u003eFertility\u003c/h4\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere were 29,864 live-births in 2025, a decrease of 11.4% from the 33,703 live-births in the previous year. Singapore's resident total fertility rate (TFR) for 2025 was 0.87 births per female, lower than 0.97 in 2024.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch4 class=\"text-h5\"\u003eMortality\u003c/h4\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA total of 26,499 deaths were registered in 2025, 0.2% higher than the 26,442 deaths in 2024. The age-standardised death rate of the resident population decreased from 5.3 per 1,000 residents in 2024 to 5.1 per 1,000 residents in 2025. Life expectancy at birth for Singapore residents was 83.9 years in 2025, up 0.2 years from 2024 and surpassing the pre-pandemic high of 83.7 years in 2019.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv style=\"margin-bottom: 24px;\"\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eAbout this Publication\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cdiv style=\"margin-bottom: 24px;\"\u003ePopulation Trends, 2026 is DOS’ annual report comprising data on population, geographical distribution, households and housing, family formation and dissolution, fertility and mortality. The report provides a statistical analysis of Singapore's population profile. It is the twenty-second edition of an annual series that puts together different aspects of demographic statistics in a single volume.\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cdiv style=\"margin-bottom: 24px;\"\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eLinks to Resources\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca class=\"cta-button\" role=\"button\" href=\"/files/5645f043-4bb7-4181-a3a8-c36da5cd1b12.pdf\"\u003eView full report\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"/files/ee585d3a-a0cd-4e8d-8821-08a1362126fb.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eInfographic\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/statistical-tables/downloadMultiple/ZMUaefcb400I0QjaMl60CQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eNon-geospatial data time series tables\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"/files/1f0956c0-7c89-4684-bde3-b45b792636af.zip\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eGeospatial data time series\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"50:T933,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eKey Highlights\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn a quarter-on-quarter basis, the Sea Freight Transport and Freight Forwarding price indices increased sharply by 25.4% and 10.2% respectively in 2Q 2026. Other indices that rose included Postal \u0026amp; Courier (7.1%), Cargo Handling (2.7%), Warehousing \u0026amp; Storage (0.6%) and Computer Consultancy \u0026amp; Information Services (0.3%). In contrast, the Accounting Services and Telecommunications Services price indices declined by 1.8% and 1.0% respectively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn a year-on-year basis, Sea Freight Transport and Freight Forwarding increased 32.6% and 14.9% respectively in 2Q 2026. Other indices that rose were Postal \u0026amp; Courier (11.6%), Cargo Handling (3.5%), Warehousing \u0026amp; Storage (3.2%), Accounting Services (1.6%) and Computer Consultancy \u0026amp; Information Services (0.1%), while the Telecommunications Services declined 0.7%.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe quarter-on-quarter and year-on-year increases in some of the indices were mainly driven by higher fuel costs and other operating cost pressures amid conflicts in the Middle East. This was particularly the case for Sea Freight Transport and Freight Forwarding, which recorded larger increases than the other indices.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eAbout this Publication\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Services Producer Price Indices (SPPIs) measure changes in the average prices of services produced by establishments, for selected industries of the Singapore economy. The prices captured reflect the amount received by a producer, exclusive of any taxes. The SPPIs are aligned to the Singapore Standard Industrial Classification.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThey are calculated using the modified Laspeyres price index formula with fixed base weights, where the weights reference period precedes the price reference period.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eLinks to Resources\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.singstat.gov.sg/files/f1d9ddb2-a125-4572-b77c-0df9a32eedac.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eFull Report\u003c/a\u003e \u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.singstat.gov.sg/files/168972bd-57ef-4c4c-8469-b6750c923dce.xlsx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eExcel File\u003c/a\u003e \u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.singstat.gov.sg/find-data/explore-data-themes/economy-prices/producer-and-international-trade-price-indices/related-information\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eRebasing of Services Producer Price Indices Information Paper\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"51:Tcbd,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eKey Highlights\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003ch3 class=\"text-h4\"\u003eSingapore Manufactured Products Price Index\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Singapore Manufactured Products Price Index (SMPPI) rose 2.5% month-on-month in Jul 2026, following declines of 0.2% and 0.7% in May 2026 and Jun 2026 respectively. The Oil index rebounded 4.3%, after declining in the previous two months, while the Non-oil index rose 2.2%, extending the increases seen in recent months.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn a year-on-year basis, the SMPPI rose 35.5% in Jul 2026, extending its upward trend and picking up from the 31.1% increase in Jun 2026. The Oil index continued to rise, increasing 39.6%. The Non-oil index rose 34.8%, extending its upward trend, with progressively stronger increases recorded in recent months.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3 class=\"text-h4\"\u003eDomestic Supply Price Index\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Domestic Supply Price Index (DSPI) rose 2.3% month-on-month in Jul 2026, following declines of 2.0% and 3.0% in May 2026 and Jun 2026 respectively. The Oil index increased 2.9%, rebounding after declines in the previous three months, while the Non-oil index rose 2.1%, extending the increases seen in recent months.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn a year-on-year basis, the DSPI rose 33.4% in Jul 2026, extending the increases seen in recent months. The Oil index continued to rise, increasing 46.4%. The Non-oil index rose 29.3%, extending its upward trend, with progressively stronger increases recorded in recent months.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe month-on-month and year-on-year increases in the SMPPI and DSPI Non-oil indices were mainly driven by higher prices of electrical machinery apparatus, including electronic integrated circuits. The increases in the SMPPI and DSPI Oil indices were mainly due to higher prices of petroleum and petroleum products, against the backdrop of the Middle East conflict.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eAbout this Publication\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Singapore Manufactured Products Price Index (SMPPI) monitors price changes of locally manufactured commodities. The weights of the index are derived from the distribution of local production by commodity in 2022. Ex-factory prices are obtained from principal manufacturers of the items concerned.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Domestic Supply Price Index (DSPI) is designed to measure the changes in the price level of either goods manufactured locally or imported, which are retained for use in the domestic economy by the government, business or household sectors. For imported products, the prices refer to those paid by importers in bringing the goods into Singapore, commonly referred to as c.i.f. prices. In the case of Singapore manufactured products, the quotations used are ex-factory prices. The weight for each commodity item is proportional to the value of its total supply or availability in the domestic market in 2022.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe 2023-based series is compiled using the 'modified Laspeyres' formula where the weight reference period of 2022 preceded the price reference period of 2023.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eLinks to Resources\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.singstat.gov.sg/files/e278b2d7-6512-4063-8718-9e0006c5a5a4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eFull Report\u003c/a\u003e \u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.singstat.gov.sg/files/39e6f8f0-f24c-48c1-a416-4895517d8c11.xls\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eExcel File\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"52:Tba3,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eKey Highlights\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003ch3 class=\"text-h4\"\u003eImport Price Index\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Import Price Index (IPI) rose 0.8% month-on-month in Jul 2026, following declines of 2.2% and 3.3% in May 2026 and Jun 2026 respectively. The Oil index increased 1.6%, after declining for three consecutive months. The Non-oil index rose 0.5%, mainly due to higher prices of electrical machinery apparatus such as electronic integrated circuits.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn a year-on-year basis, the IPI rose 13.5% in Jul 2026, extending the increases seen in recent months. The Oil index continued to rise, increasing 45.5%. The Non-oil index rose 4.8%, extending its upward trend, mainly due to higher prices of engines \u0026amp; motors, electrical machinery apparatus such as electronic integrated circuits, and jewellery.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3 class=\"text-h4\"\u003eExport Price Index\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Export Price Index (EPI) was unchanged month-on-month in Jul 2026, following a 1.8% decline in Jun 2026. The Oil index increased 0.7%, after declining in the previous two months. The Non-oil index edged down 0.2%, following the increases in recent months, mainly due to lower prices of plastics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn a year-on-year basis, the EPI rose 13.1% in Jul 2026, extending the increases seen in recent months. The Oil index continued to rise, increasing 39.3%. The Non-oil index rose 7.3%, extending its upward trend, mainly due to higher prices of electrical machinery apparatus such as electronic integrated circuits.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe month-on-month and year-on-year increases in the IPI and EPI Oil indices were mainly due to higher prices of petroleum and petroleum products, against the backdrop of the Middle East conflict.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eAbout this Publication\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Import Price Index (IPI) is used primarily as an indicator to track the price trends of imported goods into Singapore. Prices of imports, valued at CIF terms (cost, insurance and freight) are obtained monthly from major importers. The weighting pattern of the index is derived from import statistics for 2023.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Export Price Index (EPI) measures changes in the price of all exports of merchandise from Singapore, including re-exports. In general, prices are obtained from major exporters of the selected commodities. Prices used in the index computation, are the prices of goods that physically leave Singapore’s main ports of export; also termed as Free on Board (FOB) prices. The weights of the index are derived from the distribution of export by commodity in 2023.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe 2023-based series is compiled using the Laspeyres formula where the price and weight reference periods are aligned to the year 2023.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"text-h3\"\u003eLinks to Resources\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.singstat.gov.sg/files/e38916ab-50c2-47e0-a6c5-27ab509fa3ff.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eFull Report\u003c/a\u003e \u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.singstat.gov.sg/files/c6348468-eab0-435f-9817-67df7206c047.xls\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003eExcel File\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"27:[\"$\",\"$L49\",\"mfhjfmk3-2\",{\"data\":{\"id\":\"mfhjfmk3\",\"name\":\"Latest News Publications\",\"placeholder_definition\":{\"id\":\"Main\",\"name\":\"Main\"},\"widget_definition\":{\"name\":\"latest_news_publications\"},\"parameters\":{\"calendar_link\":1,\"default_open_tab\":\"news\",\"news_link\":3,\"publications_link\":7},\"collection\":\"latest_news_publications\",\"sort\":5,\"category\":\"Home Component\",\"is_layout_widget\":\"$undefined\",\"config\":{\"listingPaths\":{\"news\":\"/news/\",\"publication\":\"/publication-resources/\"}},\"serverSideLinks\":{\"calendarLink\":{\"id\":1,\"sort\":1,\"date_created\":\"2025-12-09T12:37:44.901Z\",\"date_updated\":\"2026-03-06T06:04:24.487Z\",\"team\":\"50eead3d-0bd8-48e7-b88b-a6de7e400921\",\"status\":\"Published\",\"title\":\"View Advance Release Calendar\",\"description\":\"View dates and information about upcoming data releases.\",\"icon\":\"2d3aec78-9b66-45e9-bda8-af976efa6bc2\",\"URL\":\"/data-tools-services/advance-release-calendar\",\"processedIconUrl\":\"/files/2d3aec78-9b66-45e9-bda8-af976efa6bc2.svg\"},\"newsLink\":{\"id\":3,\"sort\":3,\"date_created\":\"2025-12-09T12:37:45.577Z\",\"date_updated\":\"2026-02-24T04:06:42.837Z\",\"team\":\"50eead3d-0bd8-48e7-b88b-a6de7e400921\",\"status\":\"Published\",\"title\":\"View all News\",\"description\":null,\"icon\":\"6ae2fea2-5146-4c93-9daa-933e7bb4096b\",\"URL\":\"/news\",\"processedIconUrl\":\"/files/6ae2fea2-5146-4c93-9daa-933e7bb4096b.svg\"},\"publicationLink\":{\"id\":7,\"sort\":null,\"date_created\":\"2025-12-09T12:37:46.898Z\",\"date_updated\":\"2026-02-24T04:07:33.331Z\",\"team\":\"50eead3d-0bd8-48e7-b88b-a6de7e400921\",\"status\":\"Published\",\"title\":\"View all Publications \u0026 Resources\",\"description\":null,\"icon\":\"45335282-9e37-4b5e-91eb-c412a875e53e\",\"URL\":\"/publication-resources\",\"processedIconUrl\":\"/files/45335282-9e37-4b5e-91eb-c412a875e53e.svg\"}},\"serverSideData\":{\"newsData\":[{\"id\":\"e0d3498d-e52f-412a-85c8-1ca218f7a130\",\"title\":\"International Investment Position, 2Q 2026\",\"content\":\"$4a\",\"short_description\":\"Singapore’s net asset position in the International Investment Position rose 5.2% quarter-on-quarter to $1,577 bil as at end 2Q 2026.\",\"slug\":\"international-investment-position-2q2026\",\"published_date\":\"2026-09-30\",\"is_multi_themes\":\"single\",\"add_new_links\":[{\"id\":\"96fe058d-12b7-4337-8283-8f9275978ec7\",\"text\":\"Press Release - International Investment Position, 2Q 2026\",\"link\":\"/files/d8244be2-34c7-4a83-8342-03bb457c99b6.pdf\",\"url_type\":null,\"link_type\":\"file\",\"_original\":{\"id\":\"96fe058d-12b7-4337-8283-8f9275978ec7\",\"sort\":null,\"date_created\":\"2026-09-30T03:00:07.023Z\",\"date_updated\":null,\"team\":\"50eead3d-0bd8-48e7-b88b-a6de7e400921\",\"text\":\"Press Release - International Investment Position, 2Q 2026\",\"link_type\":\"file\",\"url_type\":null,\"internal_pages\":null,\"internal_link_file\":\"d8244be2-34c7-4a83-8342-03bb457c99b6\",\"internal_link_pages\":null,\"internal_link_news\":null,\"internal_link_publication\":null,\"internal_link_infograpic\":null,\"internal_link_sdg\":null,\"internal_link_sdg_indicator\":null,\"internal_link_quizzes\":null,\"internal_link_industry\":null,\"internal_link_sub_industry\":null,\"external_link\":null}},{\"id\":\"a8ebdd7c-d0d8-4e87-af6f-1c9138d5da78\",\"text\":\"Dashboard on Singapore International Investment Position\",\"link\":\"/publication-resources/singapore-international-investment-position-dashboard\",\"url_type\":\"internal\",\"link_type\":\"url\",\"_original\":{\"id\":\"a8ebdd7c-d0d8-4e87-af6f-1c9138d5da78\",\"sort\":null,\"date_created\":\"2026-09-30T03:00:07.121Z\",\"date_updated\":null,\"team\":\"50eead3d-0bd8-48e7-b88b-a6de7e400921\",\"text\":\"Dashboard on Singapore International Investment Position\",\"link_type\":\"url\",\"url_type\":\"internal\",\"internal_pages\":\"publication_resources\",\"internal_link_file\":null,\"internal_link_pages\":null,\"internal_link_news\":null,\"internal_link_publication\":{\"key\":\"7063d14b-1598-4d40-8d92-1c7e81853bc2\",\"collection\":\"xtr_cms_data_publication\"},\"internal_link_infograpic\":null,\"internal_link_sdg\":null,\"internal_link_sdg_indicator\":null,\"internal_link_quizzes\":null,\"internal_link_industry\":null,\"internal_link_sub_industry\":null,\"external_link\":null}},{\"id\":\"81ebd252-63bc-46a3-8ed1-c21ee82916c6\",\"text\":\"Tables on International Investment Position\",\"link\":\"https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/statistical-tables/downloadMultiple/6EqTcv5aYUpNFgjfHpBCJQ\",\"url_type\":\"external\",\"link_type\":\"url\",\"_original\":{\"id\":\"81ebd252-63bc-46a3-8ed1-c21ee82916c6\",\"sort\":null,\"date_created\":\"2026-09-30T03:00:07.161Z\",\"date_updated\":null,\"team\":\"50eead3d-0bd8-48e7-b88b-a6de7e400921\",\"text\":\"Tables on International Investment Position\",\"link_type\":\"url\",\"url_type\":\"external\",\"internal_pages\":null,\"internal_link_file\":null,\"internal_link_pages\":null,\"internal_link_news\":null,\"internal_link_publication\":null,\"internal_link_infograpic\":null,\"internal_link_sdg\":null,\"internal_link_sdg_indicator\":null,\"internal_link_quizzes\":null,\"internal_link_industry\":null,\"internal_link_sub_industry\":null,\"external_link\":\"https://tablebuilder.singstat.gov.sg/statistical-tables/downloadMultiple/6EqTcv5aYUpNFgjfHpBCJQ\"}}],\"redirect_link_field\":null,\"resource_type\":{\"xtr_cms_data_ms_value_id\":{\"text\":\"Press Releases\"}},\"subject_type\":{\"title\":\"International Accounts\",\"theme\":{\"theme_name\":\"Economy \u0026 Prices\",\"menu_icon\":{\"value\":\"icon-economy-and-prices\"}}},\"themes_type\":[],\"add_new_links_advanced\":\"$undefined\",\"layout\":{\"name\":\"DosOneColumnContentLayout\"},\"fileInfo\":{\"count\":3},\"processedTags\":[{\"icon\":\"icon-economy-and-prices\",\"label\":\"Economy \u0026 Prices\",\"alt\":\"Economy \u0026 Prices\"},{\"label\":\"International Accounts\",\"highlighted\":true}],\"alt_url\":\"\",\"isAltUrlExternal\":false},{\"id\":\"8bdab535-0ce2-4366-821a-0e26a300d9f2\",\"title\":\"Monthly Manufacturing Performance, Aug 2026\",\"content\":null,\"short_description\":\"Manufacturing output decreased 0.5% in Aug 2026 on a seasonally adjusted month-on-month basis.\",\"slug\":\"monthly-manufacturing-performance-aug2026\",\"published_date\":\"2026-09-28\",\"is_multi_themes\":\"single\",\"add_new_links\":[],\"redirect_link_field\":\"https://www.edb.gov.sg/en/corporate-news/monthly-manufacturing-performance\",\"resource_type\":{\"xtr_cms_data_ms_value_id\":{\"text\":\"Press Releases\"}},\"subject_type\":{\"title\":\"Manufacturing\",\"theme\":{\"theme_name\":\"Industry\",\"menu_icon\":{\"value\":\"icon-industry\"}}},\"themes_type\":[],\"add_new_links_advanced\":\"$undefined\",\"layout\":{\"name\":\"DosRedirectLayout\"},\"fileInfo\":{\"count\":0},\"processedTags\":[{\"icon\":\"icon-industry\",\"label\":\"Industry\",\"alt\":\"Industry\"},{\"label\":\"Manufacturing\",\"highlighted\":true}],\"alt_url\":\"https://www.edb.gov.sg/en/corporate-news/monthly-manufacturing-performance\",\"isAltUrlExternal\":true},{\"id\":\"4b6d43b3-f1e5-45a7-8ef0-81ebdd4bcd27\",\"title\":\"Singapore Consumer Price Index, Aug 2026\",\"content\":\"$4b\",\"short_description\":\"CPI in Aug 2026 rose 0.6% from the previous month and 2.3% from a year earlier.\",\"slug\":\"singapore-consumer-price-index-aug2026\",\"published_date\":\"2026-09-23\",\"is_multi_themes\":\"single\",\"add_new_links\":[{\"id\":\"59df1038-418d-4018-9353-ef75746c3445\",\"text\":\"Press Release - Singapore Consumer Price Index, Aug 2026\",\"link\":\"/files/f47df7ae-54ab-4310-8a0e-39cf4b9d9fc9.pdf\",\"url_type\":null,\"link_type\":\"file\",\"_original\":{\"id\":\"59df1038-418d-4018-9353-ef75746c3445\",\"sort\":null,\"date_created\":\"2026-09-23T05:00:08.542Z\",\"date_updated\":null,\"team\":\"50eead3d-0bd8-48e7-b88b-a6de7e400921\",\"text\":\"Press Release - Singapore Consumer Price Index, Aug 2026\",\"link_type\":\"file\",\"url_type\":null,\"internal_pages\":null,\"internal_link_file\":\"f47df7ae-54ab-4310-8a0e-39cf4b9d9fc9\",\"internal_link_pages\":null,\"internal_link_news\":null,\"internal_link_publication\":null,\"internal_link_infograpic\":null,\"internal_link_sdg\":null,\"internal_link_sdg_indicator\":null,\"internal_link_quizzes\":null,\"internal_link_industry\":nul